        {
          "key": "uuid-control|secret_identity|node.go|setNodeInterface|50",
          "matched": false,
          "verdict": "false",
          "class": "secret_identity",
          "severity": "medium",
          "file": "node.go",
          "lines": [
            50,
            53
          ],
          "symbol": "setNodeInterface",
          "title": "UUID v1/v6 random node ID lacks multicast bit, can collide with real MACs",
          "description": "Entry: node.go:39 (setNodeInterface), reached from NewUUID (version1.go:38), NewV6 (version6.go:77) and NodeID() (node.go:64) when no interface has a usable hardware address.\nAttacker: any workload or host that generates UUIDs while owning no usable hardware address; it writes a random value into the IEEE MAC namespace it does not own, crossing the boundary between randomly assigned node values and real hardware addresses.\nPath: getHardwareInterface returns \"\", nil (node_net.go:32), so setNodeInterface takes the fallback branch and calls randomBits(nodeID[:]) (node.go:52), filling nodeID with 48 random bits; version1.go:40 and version6.go:79 then copy those bits into the node field of every generated UUID with no marker that they are random.\nWhy nothing stops it: randomBits only fills bytes, and no code sets the multicast bit (the least significant bit of nodeID[0]) that RFC 4122 section 4.1.6 / RFC 9562 section 6.10 require for a randomly generated node, so the generated node is indistinguishable from a genuine MAC address and can collide with one.\nImpact for this library: the node field of a UUID produced without a hardware address can equal a real device's MAC, so two different hosts can mint the same v1/v6 UUID whenever their timestamps and 14-bit clock sequences coincide, and consumers cannot tell a random node from a hardware-derived one, defeating the uniqueness/integrity guarantee the multicast-bit rule exists to protect.\nFix: after randomBits(nodeID[:]) set the multicast bit, e.g. nodeID[0] |= 0x01, and do the same wherever a random node value is installed, so a generated node is always a random multicast address and can never collide with a real MAC."
        },
        {
          "key": "uuid-control|data_exposure|version6.go|NewV6WithTime|39",
          "matched": false,
          "verdict": "real",
          "class": "data_exposure",
          "severity": "medium",
          "file": "version6.go",
          "lines": [
            39,
            45
          ],
          "symbol": "NewV6WithTime",
          "title": "Unsynchronized getTime call in NewV6WithTime yields duplicate v6 UUIDs",
          "description": "NewV6WithTime (version6.go:39-45) calls getTime() without holding timeMu, while GetTime, ClockSequence, SetClockSequence, and getV7Time all lock it. This data race on clockSeq/lasttime can produce duplicate v6 UUIDs."
        }
      ],
